WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.195
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:05.070
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
I'm Peter-Mark Verwoerd.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.189
I'm a Solutions Architect
here at Google Cloud,

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:13.380
and I specialize in
helping customers migrate.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.000
So I go and see a
lot of customers

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.200
as they made the decision
to come to Google Cloud

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.080
or in the process about thinking
about moving to Google Cloud.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.850
I help educate them
about various aspects

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.210
of the cloud and kind of
what advantages it can offer.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.270
And also, in particular,
how to take what they have

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.390
and what they are using
and migrate that over.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.900
In the course of what I do, a
question that comes up a lot

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.490
is, OK, great.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.750
How much does this cost?

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.000
What is this going to cost me?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.240
What's the bottom line?

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.600
And I think that we tend to
focus these conversations

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.659
particularly on data processing
and data processing pipelines.

00:00:49.659 --> 00:00:51.450
Because I think those
are more conceptually

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.130
difficult to price out.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:58.170
I think to a certain extent,
if you understand the way

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.930
that, say, a web front
end or a database

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.811
works and the capacity
that it needs to meet,

00:01:03.811 --> 00:01:05.310
that it's relatively
straightforward

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.142
to be able to price
that out in the cloud,

00:01:07.142 --> 00:01:09.600
even if you're not entirely
familiar with that environment.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.330
But data processing
pipelines tend

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.460
to bring in so many
different kinds of products

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.918
and so many different
moving pieces

00:01:15.918 --> 00:01:19.754
that it tends to be a lot more
difficult. It's not impossible,

00:01:19.754 --> 00:01:21.420
but it tends to be a
lot more difficult.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.080
So a lot of our conversations
end up focusing on that.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.860
And so what I'd
like to do right now

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.040
is just walk through what
those conversations are

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.920
like that I go through
with customers that I meet.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.110
And kind of help you
out and giving you

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.260
some sort of context
and guidelines

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.402
towards doing this on your own.

00:01:40.402 --> 00:01:41.860
But first, I just
want to start out

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.570
with a relatively
simple metaphor.

00:01:46.962 --> 00:01:49.189
What am I pointing to?

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:50.230
I am pointing to nothing.

00:01:53.218 --> 00:01:54.720
There.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.240
So think for a
second if you could

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.300
try to purchase
energy for your home

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.804
for like the next three
years all in once.

00:02:06.804 --> 00:02:09.470
Do you think it'd be possible to
kind of get that number exactly

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:09.982
right?

00:02:09.982 --> 00:02:12.440
Do you think you could do it
efficiently so that you're not

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.097
overpaying?

00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:15.680
Do you think you could do it in
such a way that would actually

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.710
make sense?

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.760
I mean, like what would happen
if you bought not enough energy

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.237
or you bought way too
much and your lights

00:02:22.237 --> 00:02:23.070
are on all the time?

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.210
So this is why I
think companies that

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:30.920
are approaching the cloud are
sometimes confused by the way

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:31.670
things are priced.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.190
The way products are
priced is that it's

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.140
fundamentally different
from the way people

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.500
are used to pricing out
infrastructure and pricing

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.380
out resources.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.431
It can be very confusing.

00:02:41.431 --> 00:02:43.430
This, by the way, is the
head of the SA program.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.130
This is a guy named Miles Ward.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.980
We frequently make
GIFs of each other

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.811
and use them in presentations
because I think it's hilarious.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.690
So the reason why
it's different,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.770
and the reason why I
use the energy analogy,

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.596
is because cloud pricing
is utility pricing.

00:03:03.596 --> 00:03:04.720
It's based on what you use.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.790
It's not something that
is pre-provisioned.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.540
And I think that's a mind
shift from somebody who's

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.590
coming in with a expectation
of the way things are purchased

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.180
inside a data center.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.560
And so if you look at
the way cloud pricing is,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.580
if you're familiar with
how data center works,

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.389
I think it can be
somewhat confusing.

00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:25.930
So if you approach
this from somebody

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.820
who's got a good
understanding, like

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.410
I know how to price out in
the next three to five years,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.490
whatever that is, within a
reasonable amount of accuracy

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.990
given a certain
amount of growth.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:37.510
But I come and look at
what the cloud offers.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:39.950
That is different from
what I'm looking at.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.700
So if you look at
things like services

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.920
that are charged
by, say, the amount

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.730
of storage per month, things
that have streaming and batch

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:52.330
processing, and then
products with storage

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.970
and streaming and
querying pricing,

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.120
this is all very different.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.720
It's all bundled together
in very, very different ways

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.970
if you're used to being
able to purchase something

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:02.532
as a physical device.

00:04:02.532 --> 00:04:04.240
And so if you come to
that and you think,

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.115
does this make sense?

00:04:05.115 --> 00:04:06.580
If somebody asks
you, like here's

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.621
how Cloud Storage, here's
how BigQuery is priced.

00:04:08.621 --> 00:04:10.570
Does this makes sense?

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:13.230
Sometimes, maybe, actually
I'm not really sure.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.980
And so these are the
kind of conversations

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.740
we want to have with our
customers and say like,

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.709
this is how you can make
sense of what this is

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.680
and how you can make sense
of what we're doing and how

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.540
that maps to what
you're used to.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:33.420
And so we take the
conversation through to,

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.600
this is where a lot of
data center pricing is

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:43.000
and how you should see that
relate to what the cloud does.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.120
So there are generally two ways
that you can purchase capacity

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:46.840
in a data center.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.570
The first way is regular
capacity planning.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.320
You have some sort of regular
timeframe where you buy things.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.020
You have a certain
amount of capacity

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.534
that you buy during
that timeframe.

00:04:57.534 --> 00:04:59.950
Whether it's by some sort of
metric, some sort of decision

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:00.530
process.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.010
Whatever it is, it's a regular
path that people follow.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.704
The other way is
when you fall out

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.370
of that pattern where
there is some sort

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.580
of out-of-band purchase
for whatever reason

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.660
and you're making
something off-schedule.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.240
But purchasing capacity
for a data center

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.040
isn't just buying new servers.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.430
It's buying storage media,
buying racks, power,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.940
networking, routers, switches,
whatever the case may be.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.130
Yeah, space even.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.010
Hiring new people.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.980
These are all things that--

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.740
oh, thank you.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.940
These are all things that are
affected by the purchasing

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:34.570
that you make.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.960
Does that work?

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:40.720
It seems like not.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:43.780
In any case.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.250
What's wrong with that?

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.630
I mean, there are
lots of customers

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:51.690
and there are a lot
of companies out there

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:52.815
that are highly successful.

00:05:52.815 --> 00:05:54.600
They're running in data centers.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.174
That's not connected.

00:05:56.174 --> 00:05:57.094
OK.

00:05:57.094 --> 00:05:58.260
And they're doing just fine.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.280
People are running highly
successful businesses in just--

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.390
oh.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:02.890
OK.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:04.230
I'm sorry about that.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.730
It works.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:06.230
Oh, yeah.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:07.410
It does.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.500
So there are lots of companies
that are kind of highly

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.610
successful at doing this.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.450
And so why kind of change
the way things are priced?

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.750
And I think at
Google Cloud, we feel

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.610
like this method of
purchasing is not ideal.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:23.830
Let's kind of talk about why.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.200
So in the first scenario
why this might not be ideal,

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.032
let's say you're on
a development team.

00:06:29.032 --> 00:06:30.240
You've created a new product.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.530
You've created a new
feature, whatever that is.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.730
You need extra capacity.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.230
You reach out to
operations and say,

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.030
we need x kind of capacity.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.790
And the answer is hopefully yes.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.980
And hopefully being
able to deploy something

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.260
isn't the best strategy.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.355
The worst answer of
course, is rather

00:06:45.355 --> 00:06:47.730
than getting a hopefully yes,
you can get definitely not.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.680
We're out of capacity
until the next cycle.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.430
That's also bad.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.246
Because then whatever
you plan on releasing

00:06:52.246 --> 00:06:54.120
to your customers,
whatever needs to come out

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.890
there, whatever work
your team has done

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:56.860
is not going out there.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:58.660
And so that's not
a great experience.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.740
On the other hand, if you're
doing capacity planning,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.870
the two scenarios you
see your capacity at

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.610
is either you are
under-provisioned when

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.943
you've purchased something
new and people have not fully

00:07:07.943 --> 00:07:09.789
moved in yet or you
are overcapacity

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:10.830
or you're oversubscribed.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.924
People are not able to
use as much capacity

00:07:12.924 --> 00:07:15.090
as they would like to because
you don't have enough.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.730
And neither of those
scenarios are ideal.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.730
In the out-of-band purchase,
there are similar issues.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.340
Except what you're
coming across now

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.860
is because this is unusual,
because this is not planned,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.500
each one of these is kind
of this own unique snowflake

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:29.824
of an issue.

00:07:29.824 --> 00:07:31.990
And so a development team
has created something new.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.020
Hey, management says
this has to go out now.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:34.950
We need the capacity.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.600
It's got to support
a million users.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.250
And operations says, OK.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.790
What does a million users mean
in terms of how many cores

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:41.540
do we have to buy?

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.320
How much storage do we need?

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.670
And it depends dramatically
on the use case.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:49.592
And so you have this point
where either operations

00:07:49.592 --> 00:07:52.050
needs to translate what the
development team has told them.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.010
And hopefully, come up
with accurate numbers

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:57.090
as to what that might mean
for the hardware to purchase.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:58.830
Or, the development
team says, we're

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:02.370
going to guess that what
we need is this much cores

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:03.480
and this much RAM.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.630
And we think we're right.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.240
And that translation
is not easy, even

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:07.740
for people with a
lot of experience.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:08.730
It can go wrong.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.160
And it's frequently
difficult to do.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.020
Or worse yet, maybe
you've created a feature

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.900
and nobody has any idea
how much capacity you need.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.374
This is something totally new.

00:08:17.374 --> 00:08:18.540
We're testing something out.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.990
We could be totally wrong.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:21.910
We have no idea what to ask for.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.375
Or, again, you have somebody
who says, you know what?

00:08:24.375 --> 00:08:26.000
This is going to be
the hot, new thing.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:26.520
We're going to do great.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.644
We're going to need millions
and millions of users.

00:08:28.644 --> 00:08:31.140
And it comes out and actually,
not that many people.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.820
So you've got all
this wasted capacity.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.169
The other end of the spectrum,
it's like, you know what?

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:35.010
It's a tiny, little feature.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.150
No one will ever see it.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.100
And suddenly, it becomes
the next new thing.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.890
So all these sorts of things
lead to problems with the way

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.299
that purchasing happens.

00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:45.390
And so we think
at Google Cloud we

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.760
can try to help change
the way people think

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.560
about purchasing what
they need, purchasing

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.370
infrastructure and supporting
the applications that they

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.250
want to deploy.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:55.750
So

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:58.660
So we designed
Google Cloud pricing

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.490
to be as flexible for
customers as possible.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:07.090
We're not trying to make you
commit to long-term pricing,

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:09.730
to choose machine
types, to choose zones,

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:10.810
to choose regions.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:13.310
We want to be flexible
for customers.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.660
So we've done a number of
things that kind of enable this.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:20.950
One of them is
sustained use discounts,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:25.240
which give you a discount
at the end of the month,

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.760
depending on how long you've
run your virtual machines

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.484
without any
interaction from you.

00:09:29.484 --> 00:09:30.400
We're just saying, OK.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:31.630
You used it for this long.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:32.838
This is what the discount is.

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:34.270
It just happens.

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:37.000
Things like preemptable
virtual machines,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.040
which are great for
batch, and interruptible

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.420
workloads where you
just need more capacity

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.230
but have the ability
to be able to stand

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:45.690
to lose a worker or two.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.190
Those are great because you can
run these in data processing

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:48.689
pipelines.

00:09:48.689 --> 00:09:51.280
Require a lot of
capacity, but you

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:52.750
can stand to lose some things.

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.820
And it just keeps
going, picking up

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.820
new preemptable virtual
machines at something

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.810
like 80% of the cost of a
regular virtual machine.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.236
And finally, permanent billing.

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:06.860
So actually, billing you by the
minute for the virtual machines

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.530
you're running so that you're
really only just paying

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:11.570
for the things you use,
which is kind of the way

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:15.180
we think cloud pricing
should work inherently.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.190
Custom machine types is
something interesting

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.650
that we launched,
I think last year.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.000
Maybe the year before.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.730
Because we saw a lot of
customers asking, look.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:27.480
We've got these workloads
that say I need 20, 24 cores

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.430
to run x workload.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.350
And you've got 16 core machines.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.100
You've got 32.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.930
So I'm constantly
over-provisioning,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.013
spending a lot more money
than I actually need to.

00:10:36.013 --> 00:10:38.310
And we kind of don't
want you to do that.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:40.710
Everything at Google runs
on top of a container.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.334
So our virtual machines actually
also run on top of containers.

00:10:43.334 --> 00:10:45.960
It actually gives us a lot of
flexibility in how we design

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:48.540
and how we build out
our virtual machines.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:52.650
So we said, well, we have a
standard set of instance types.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.230
We feel like they meet a
lot of general use cases.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.100
If they don't meet your use
case, then design it yourself.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.950
You can put together
what kind of--

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.176
however much CPU, however
much RAM you need.

00:11:04.176 --> 00:11:06.300
And then you can actually
rightsize your workloads.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.550
And what we've seen is it's
had a huge impact where

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.870
a lot of customers say, OK,
this actually fits my workload.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:12.990
I am not over-provisioned.

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.490
I'm not paying for
more than I need.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:18.180
And like the slide says,
up to like 19% savings

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.720
versus before when you were
constantly over-provisioning

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.251
if you weren't quite where we
created these standard instance

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:25.750
types.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:31.730
We constantly support as much
of the open source community

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:32.535
as we possibly can.

00:11:32.535 --> 00:11:34.410
We are big fans of the
open source community.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.320
We contribute to it a lot.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.960
Things like TensorFlow,
like Apache Beam,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:42.290
like our white papers for
MapReduce and Bigtable

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:43.560
and all these sorts of things.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.809
And the reason we do that
is because we know customers

00:11:45.809 --> 00:11:47.660
are using a lot of
these third-party tools,

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:49.250
a lot of open source tools.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:52.249
Everyone, everywhere is using
something somewhere like that.

00:11:52.249 --> 00:11:54.040
And we want to be able
to support everyone.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.150
And what we don't want
to do is have a platform

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:59.840
where you're using our platform
and staying on our platform

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.570
because you cant leave because
you've got proprietary APIs,

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:07.880
because you've got proprietary
inoperable or interchangeable

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.160
services with something
else that you want to use.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.230
And so if you bring in your
data to Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.380
if you bring into Dataproc,
if you bring into BigQuery,

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:15.650
and then one day you
want to move it out.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:16.010
You know what?

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:17.570
Actually, BigQuery's
not doing it for me.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:18.890
I want to use this other tool.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.530
Bigtable isn't quite
right for this use case.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:21.980
I'm going to drop
it into Cassandra.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:23.420
We want to be able to
support that and have

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:24.410
the interoperability.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:26.180
We don't want to
keep you locked in.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:27.650
We want you to use
Google Cloud because you

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:28.691
want to use Google Cloud.

00:12:28.691 --> 00:12:32.200
Because you find it
the most advantageous.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:33.720
So it should be
kind of obvious now

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.150
that the way we consider
pricing and the way

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.082
we price things is based
entirely on how you use it

00:12:39.082 --> 00:12:41.040
and for how long you use
it and how much you're

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.790
consuming rather than asking you
to ever kind of pre-provision

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:46.650
and pre-approve or
try to figure out

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:51.100
in advance how much capacity
you're going to be using.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.960
So within that lens,
I think the difference

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.029
between buying physical hardware
and managing, say, a data

00:12:58.029 --> 00:12:59.820
center, managing racks,
and all those sorts

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:04.290
of things, versus how you manage
capacity planning in the cloud

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.950
starts to make a lot more sense.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.120
And so with that in
mind, what I'd like to do

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.240
is just walk through a few
of the-- well, not a few.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.390
Most of the data
processing-related services

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:16.050
that we have.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:18.270
And kind of talk about
the way they're priced.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:22.470
And talk about ways that
we see customers kind

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.100
of looking for
particular cost drivers

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.150
in how they're being used.

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:31.050
And again, the reason why I
like to focus on data processing

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:34.170
as opposed to, say, compute
like Container Engine or Compute

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:37.074
Engine, or so on, is I think
because so many of these things

00:13:37.074 --> 00:13:37.740
tie-in together.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:40.140
And that data
processing environments

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:43.424
tend to be much more
complex, that it's

00:13:43.424 --> 00:13:45.840
important to understand how
these are individually priced,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.950
and then how they're
priced together.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.340
So the first one I'm going
to talk about is Pub/Sub.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:55.159
Pub/Sub is scalable
messaging, push/pull globally.

00:13:55.159 --> 00:13:56.700
This is another one
of these services

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.370
that Google uses
extensively internally.

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.227
We use Pub/Sub
messaging constantly.

00:14:02.227 --> 00:14:03.810
And so this is the
same service that's

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.270
exposed to Cloud customers.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:08.109
Pub/Sub recently
changed its pricing

00:14:08.109 --> 00:14:09.900
to be only based on
storage, which actually

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.010
makes this pretty easy.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.570
There are no multiple
dimensions to watch.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:13.980
It's nothing for me
to really explain.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:14.688
It's all storage.

00:14:14.688 --> 00:14:18.870
So how much storage are you
actually consuming on Pub/Sub?

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:22.970
And so it's not
pre-provisioning topics.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:24.740
It's not pre-provisioning
messages.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.230
It's not pre-provisioning
kind of anything.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:29.510
It's how much storage are
you putting out on Pub/Sub?

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:32.700
And that's the real cost driver.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:36.100
Cloud storage, scalable
object storage.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:37.850
Similar to Pub/Sub,
it's based on how much

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:38.766
storage are you using.

00:14:38.766 --> 00:14:40.400
How much data are
you putting in there?

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:41.650
And that's how much we charge.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:44.370
We're not going to, again, ask
you to pre-provision anything.

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:45.600
It's just, what is in there?

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.430
What goes out there?

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:49.620
The thing to watch
for Cloud Storage

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.170
is there are multiple
storage classes.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.960
And this is
interesting and useful

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.640
because each of
these storage classes

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.760
have different use cases
and useful use cases,

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.310
but it depends on what
you're using them for.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.470
Multi-regional,
like the slide says,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:08.250
it's really good for lowering
the latency to end users,

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:12.327
to getting your data to end
users as quickly as possible

00:15:12.327 --> 00:15:13.410
from your storage buckets.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:14.430
It's not about caching.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.900
But actually, pushing out
unique data to your end users

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.457
as quickly as possible.

00:15:18.457 --> 00:15:20.040
Those are available
across continents.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.640
So you can have multi-regional
US, multi-regional Europe,

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:25.020
multi-regional Asia.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.791
And it will spread the
data around those areas.

00:15:27.791 --> 00:15:28.290
Yes.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.582
AUDIENCE: Can I ask a question?

00:15:29.582 --> 00:15:32.081
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Could you
just remember it for the end,

00:15:32.081 --> 00:15:32.594
please?

00:15:32.594 --> 00:15:34.760
Could you remember the
question for the end, please?

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:35.630
We will have a Q&amp;A.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:36.850
AUDIENCE: That's fine.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:39.380
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Thank you.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:42.800
So near line and cold
line are varying levels

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.931
of archival storage, depending
on how frequently are they

00:15:45.931 --> 00:15:46.430
accessed.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:50.030
And then subsequently, they're
cheaper depending on that.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:52.326
Regional storage is kind
of what it sounds like.

00:15:52.326 --> 00:15:53.450
It stays within the region.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.060
And I think for most data
processing pipelines,

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.460
regional storage is what
most customers end up

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:01.100
using because a lot
of data processing

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.240
involves data locality.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:03.980
So you want your
data to be nearby

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:07.170
so it can interact with various
services as you use them.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:10.280
So regional storage tends
to be what customers end up

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.091
using for data processing.

00:16:12.091 --> 00:16:13.340
This one, I will have to read.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:16.520
So for some
perspective, a terabyte

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:21.380
in regional storage in the
US costs $20.48 for 1 month.

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:27.110
And a petabyte for 1
month is $20,971.52.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:30.760
So just some range
of numbers there.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:36.160
So Cloud Bigtable is a scalable
high-speed NoSQL Database.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.860
It's priced essentially
across two dimensions,

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:41.320
across how many nodes
you've provisioned

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:44.630
and how much storage
you're actually using.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:49.800
So the important things to
note here, there are three.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:54.870
One, you have to provision
at least three nodes.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:17:00.000
And two, the storage can be
either SSD or spinning disk,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.760
hard drive disks.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:04.530
And three, like the
balance between-- this

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:07.180
is one of the few cases where
you do actually pre-provision.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:08.910
Is the balance between
pre provisioning

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:15.690
nodes for query capacity
or operational capacity

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:19.140
versus storage, which is
just build as you consume it.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:24.900
So when we get questions about,
how do you price out Bigtable?

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:26.204
What's the big cost driver?

00:17:26.204 --> 00:17:28.079
Unfortunately, this is
kind of where guidance

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:29.287
gets a little more difficult.

00:17:29.287 --> 00:17:31.990
Because as a database, it's got
a huge variety of use cases.

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:34.410
So it's going to
depend significantly

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:37.120
on how you use it.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:40.410
But some loose guidelines.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:42.690
If you find that
you're using Bigtable

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.600
to drive a lot of
operations, you're

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.906
doing a lot of reads
and writes, then

00:17:47.906 --> 00:17:49.530
the number of nodes
that you have tends

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.547
to be the big cost driver.

00:17:51.547 --> 00:17:53.880
If, on the other hand, you
are storing a lot of data and

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:55.666
you're not doing a
lot of operations.

00:17:55.666 --> 00:17:58.040
Then, obviously, storage is
going to be more significant.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:01.190
But that might be a scenario
where you start considering,

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:04.260
is Bigtable the right
choice for this use case?

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:07.860
And this is a little
tangential from this talk,

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:11.280
but it comes up
so often, is there

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:12.530
are a lot of storage products.

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:14.029
There are a lot of
database products

00:18:14.029 --> 00:18:15.390
available on Google Cloud.

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:17.550
Which one's the right
one for what I'm doing?

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:19.008
Because it seems
like a few of them

00:18:19.008 --> 00:18:21.290
could actually do this use case.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:22.390
Could cover this use case.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:23.912
And the answer is
often, well, yeah.

00:18:23.912 --> 00:18:24.870
Actually, you're right.

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.536
A few of them could
cover that use case.

00:18:26.536 --> 00:18:28.524
And so sometimes,
the data actually

00:18:28.524 --> 00:18:30.690
tells you fairly obviously
what you should be doing.

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:31.273
You know what?

00:18:31.273 --> 00:18:33.390
I need something that's
strongly consistent.

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:35.610
I need relatable tables.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.980
And I only need it to
be in this one zone.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:40.200
OK, that's a SQL database.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.184
I don't really have
relatable data.

00:18:42.184 --> 00:18:44.850
I've got something that's super,
super high-speed, lots of reads

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:45.270
and writes.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:46.603
I'm not going to be [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:46.603 --> 00:18:47.910
OK, that's probably Bigtable.

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:49.470
But there's a lot of
use cases that kind of

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.636
fall in the gray areas, in
between different product

00:18:51.636 --> 00:18:52.170
types.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:55.050
And so figuring out
which one to use

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.390
and which one's the
best for each scenario

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:00.659
can actually can be-- it can
be difficult to figure out

00:19:00.659 --> 00:19:02.700
without knowing more about
the specific use case.

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:05.550
And that usually generally comes
down to a bit of experience,

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:08.920
a bit of best practices, and
a bit of experimentation.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:10.890
So now, this discussion
about whether you

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:13.290
should be using
Bigtable or data store

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.060
if you're storing a lot of data
but not doing a lot of traffic.

00:19:16.060 --> 00:19:18.455
It's going to depend a lot
on what a lot of traffic

00:19:18.455 --> 00:19:19.080
actually means.

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:24.510
I'm sorry.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.950
So anyway.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.760
Final part about Bigtable
is the cost driver

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:31.230
is in terms of storage.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:34.080
Are you using SSDs or are
you using hard drives?

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.690
If you've ever purchased
storage before for pretty much

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.070
anything, it's the same rule.

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:42.440
SSDs perform more quickly,
but are more expensive.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:46.080
So Dataproc is a managed
Hadoop and SPARC service.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:50.220
So it is the same Hadoop that
you could download and run

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:51.140
your own.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:52.140
It's just managed by us.

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.240
We put it on top of
virtual machines.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.620
Dataproc, the pricing
is very straightforward.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:00.624
It is just a additional charge
on top of the virtual machines

00:20:00.624 --> 00:20:01.790
that are running underneath.

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:05.250
So the regular Compute
Engine charges remain,

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.350
and then we put an
additional charge

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.860
on for how much
Dataproc you're using.

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:15.830
So again, finding out
or trying to explain

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:18.480
what is a typical use case on
Dataproc is kind of difficult.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:21.470
There's a lot of versatility
to what you can do with Hadoop.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:23.540
A lot of different use cases.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:25.250
And so having
specific guidelines

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.500
tends to be very difficult
unless you know more

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:28.610
about the use case.

00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:31.240
Broadly, really, really
broadly speaking,

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.980
if they are temporary clusters.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.860
If they are transient
clusters, the number of cores

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.290
tends to be the
biggest cost driver.

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:40.940
If it's a permanent
cluster, it tends

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:43.400
to actually skew the other
way and the amount of storage

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:45.170
that you have provisioned
for the cluster.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:49.120
But again, it's highly
dependent on use case.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:52.610
Cloud Dataflow is our
next-generation stream

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.519
and batch processing tool.

00:20:54.519 --> 00:20:56.060
Again, this is based
on a lot of work

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:57.184
that we've done internally.

00:20:57.184 --> 00:20:59.780
Based on MillWheel
and FlumeJava.

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.660
And this tends to be more of
the kind of data processing work

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:04.355
that Google uses internally.

00:21:04.355 --> 00:21:09.305
Dataflow prices in two
kind of different cases.

00:21:09.305 --> 00:21:11.270
It prices whether you're
running a batch job

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.070
or whether you're
running a streaming job.

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:14.750
And within that, then
it just prices on,

00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:16.416
what are the resources
you're consuming?

00:21:16.416 --> 00:21:18.860
What are you consuming in terms
of cores, in terms of RAM,

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:20.990
in terms of storage?

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:23.690
So when using
Dataflow, again, this

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:26.120
is going to sound a bit
repetitive because it's

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:29.030
the same sort of story
as it is for Dataproc.

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:31.400
It depends so much on
the kind of use case.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.860
And with ETL tools, with data
processing tools, especially

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.910
things that sit so core to
what so many companies do,

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:46.250
the kinds of prices that it can
drive vary very, very wildly.

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:48.470
So it's kind of hard to
come up with very, very

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:49.220
specific guidance.

00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:50.930
But loosely speaking,
what you want

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:53.630
to look at when you're adding
Dataflow to a particular data

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:56.780
processing pipeline is whether
this is going to be batch

00:21:56.780 --> 00:21:58.040
or it's going to be streaming.

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:00.970
And also, important to
note and kind of nice,

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:03.510
Dataflow also charges
by the minute.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.150
So by the minute
for the resources

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:08.540
that you're actually consuming.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:09.240
Big Query.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:16.680
So Big Query is Google's
no ops, massively scalable

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:17.760
data warehouse.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.510
That's actually also one of
our older Cloud products.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:26.490
And also, used extensively
internally as Dremel.

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:29.810
Other than that, it's actually
almost exactly the same thing.

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:33.254
So BigQuery essentially
charges you on two dimensions.

00:22:33.254 --> 00:22:34.920
On the amount of
storage you're actually

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.711
using and the amount
of queries that you're

00:22:36.711 --> 00:22:39.180
running, which is actually
really straightforward.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.900
So storage, it's
what you put in.

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:44.690
It's what you're consuming.

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:46.110
No pre-provisioning.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.510
Except that if data
remains untouched

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:51.540
for an extended period of
time, BigQuery actually

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:54.232
ages it into cheaper storage
automatically for you

00:22:54.232 --> 00:22:56.190
Queries are, again,
relatively straightforward.

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:58.648
It's charged on the number of
queries that you're-- or that

00:22:58.648 --> 00:23:02.430
the amount of data that you're
run per query is the amount

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:03.940
you're charged.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:06.866
And so that's relatively
straightforward to understand.

00:23:06.866 --> 00:23:09.240
Although, a lot of customers
approaching BigQuery kind of

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.590
don't have a good sense
of, well, how much data do

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:12.540
my queries consume?

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:14.165
And so there's a bit
of experimentation

00:23:14.165 --> 00:23:16.110
that has to happen there.

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:21.330
There is one other thing with
BigQuery in that we have also

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:25.800
added flat-rate pricing, which
basically means that you can

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:28.109
query as much as
you want per month

00:23:28.109 --> 00:23:29.650
and you're just
paying one flat rate.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:31.380
So if you've got a scenario
where you're using a lot,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:32.530
a lot of queries.

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:38.240
Say you've created a data set or
tables for a group of analysts.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.230
Then, you can say,
you know what, guys?

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:41.850
We're not even going to worry
about how many queries you're

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:42.540
going to make.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:43.110
Go nuts.

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:46.200
We've done flat-rate pricing.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.510
Generally speaking, the
cost drivers kind of

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:51.360
depend on what you're
using BigQuery for.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:54.090
We see broadly about two models.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:59.250
One where BigQuery is constantly
having lots and lots of data

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.540
added to it, more
and more over time.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:04.320
And queries kind of
happen in line with that.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:07.740
But generally, the data tends
to drive the price there more.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:09.810
The opposite side
is where you've

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:13.830
got data that's relatively
fixed in terms of how long

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:17.190
it's there, but lots
and lots of queries.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.500
And so more of an
analytical use case.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:23.970
Then, the queries tend to
drive the costs there more.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:29.190
So that's just an overview
over the different products

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.670
that we have available in
terms of data processing.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:35.130
There's, obviously, a lot
more that we could go into.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:37.171
There are a lot of databases
that I didn't really

00:24:37.171 --> 00:24:43.770
touch on, like Cloud
SQL, Spanner, data store.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:45.450
But I feel like the
ones that we covered

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:47.850
are really the ones that are
very essential and almost

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:52.590
always used in some capacity
in data processing pipelines.

00:24:52.590 --> 00:25:00.880
So while that's interesting,
I feel like it's not really

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:04.330
entirely useful to talk about
products in the abstract

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:07.180
unless you can talk about
how they all work together.

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:09.250
And I feel like putting
products together

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:12.520
in how they work is really
one of the more useful things

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:13.270
to do.

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:16.780
And so one of the missions of
the solution architecture team

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:19.357
is to help customers
figure out how

00:25:19.357 --> 00:25:21.190
to glue all the different
products together.

00:25:21.190 --> 00:25:22.750
And actually, that's--
generally speaking,

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:23.770
for most of the
people on my team,

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.080
that's actually what we
really super-enjoy doing.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:28.350
It's Not talking
about x, y, z product,

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:30.850
but talking about how all these
three products work together

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.010
to make something awesome.

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:34.810
And so what I'd like
to do now is actually

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.460
walk through a few--

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:38.710
three example
architectures focused

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:41.050
on kind of data processing.

00:25:41.050 --> 00:25:43.130
I got these architectures
from my colleagues.

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:47.567
And we have a kiosk,
actually, out on the floor.

00:25:47.567 --> 00:25:48.400
On all three floors.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:49.200
So there's one here.

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:50.533
There's one on the second floor.

00:25:50.533 --> 00:25:53.380
And there are two downstairs
in the main expo hall.

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:54.580
We call them treehouse.

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.650
So basically, you can
go in there and search

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.690
or click around
for a whole variety

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.737
of use cases,
different industries,

00:26:00.737 --> 00:26:01.570
different verticals.

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:03.350
Start clicking into
specific use cases

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:05.200
and a lot of
architectures pop up,

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:08.080
details, pricing, why you
use this, why you use that.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:10.490
And there's always
a member of our team

00:26:10.490 --> 00:26:14.780
sitting there or standing there
to explain how they're used.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:17.077
So I'm going to
steal three of those

00:26:17.077 --> 00:26:18.160
and walk you through them.

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:21.320
This picture, by the way,
was about nine months ago,

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:24.430
we had a bit of a
team meeting face

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:28.090
to face, which we rarely get
to do because we're pretty

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.430
well spread around the globe.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:30.430
And that was in Seattle.

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:33.830
And that's me in the corner
not paying attention.

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:35.580
So the first one we're
going to go through

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:37.290
is a very common
one that we see.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.420
It's called log processing.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:42.720
It's relatively straightforward.

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.090
But even with that
being said, we're

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:46.740
still using several
different data

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:53.350
processing services to
collect and analyze the data.

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.230
So how do you price
something like this?

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:57.761
Approaching this, how
do you price that out?

00:26:57.761 --> 00:26:59.760
Because you're using so
many different services.

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.280
But actually, when
you can look at this

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:05.050
is that it only comes down to
about two different things.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:10.916
First, how quickly are the logs
coming into this architecture

00:27:10.916 --> 00:27:12.290
is going to be a
big cost driver.

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:15.950
And then secondly, how
much storage are you using?

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:19.010
So the inbound data,
the streaming data,

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:20.810
is going to be
really affected by

00:27:20.810 --> 00:27:22.370
or is going to really affect--

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.220
log collection is going to
affect the streaming or batch

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:30.290
processing in Dataflow and
the ingest into BigQuery.

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:32.019
The storage, on
the other hand, is

00:27:32.019 --> 00:27:34.560
going to be the big cost driver
for Cloud Storage, obviously.

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:36.680
But the nature of
this environment

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.420
means that it's
actually relatively

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:39.710
transient on cloud storage.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:41.230
It's not staying
there long term.

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:43.700
The storage is really
coming out of BigQuery.

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:46.077
And so even though we're using
several different services

00:27:46.077 --> 00:27:47.660
here, you can actually
break this down

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:49.360
into just kind of
two broad buckets.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:52.160
How quick is the rate of ingest?

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:56.900
And that's going to affect
the cost of Pub/Sub,

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.850
the cost of Dataflow.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:00.509
And how much
storage am I putting

00:28:00.509 --> 00:28:01.550
into BigQuery in the end?

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.790
And the second one is
time series analysis.

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:10.940
This one is a
little more complex.

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:13.202
But actually, looking
at it the same way

00:28:13.202 --> 00:28:14.660
that we looked at
the previous one,

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:16.190
I think we can start
breaking it down.

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:17.981
So the first important
point is that it's--

00:28:17.981 --> 00:28:20.510
similarly to the first
one, the rate of ingest

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:23.540
is going to be a
very big cost driver

00:28:23.540 --> 00:28:27.440
into looking at what its
effects on Pub/Sub and Dataflow.

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:30.890
Now, the second thing is
what's going on down here.

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:33.262
The downstream end
of this architecture

00:28:33.262 --> 00:28:34.970
is going to be a
significant cost driver.

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:38.796
It looks-- well, I mean,
it is very complicated.

00:28:38.796 --> 00:28:40.670
There are a lot of
different things going on.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.128
Basically, because
you can interact

00:28:42.128 --> 00:28:44.254
with a lot of our different
tools in different ways

00:28:44.254 --> 00:28:45.794
to get different
results out of them.

00:28:45.794 --> 00:28:48.260
Whether you're using machine
learning to run predictions.

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:51.050
Whether you're using data lab
to do more interactive queries

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:53.780
for, say, data scientists.

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:55.730
If you're using a
database to do queries.

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:57.980
Whatever the case may be,
there's a lot of versatility

00:28:57.980 --> 00:28:59.670
and a lot of interplay
between there.

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:00.950
But despite all that.

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:04.410
So we're using six separate
services right there

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:05.807
at the far end there.

00:29:05.807 --> 00:29:07.640
Really, it actually
comes down to two things

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.170
that you need to
concern yourself with.

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:10.586
Because all you're
doing right now

00:29:10.586 --> 00:29:12.530
is you're analyzing and storing.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:15.350
So analysis is driven primarily
by how much compute you're

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:16.072
consuming.

00:29:16.072 --> 00:29:17.030
And storage is storage.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:21.290
So are you doing a
lot more analytics

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:23.240
relative to how
much you're storing?

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:26.210
In which case, the compute
cost of that far end

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.520
is going to be a
bigger cost driver.

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:31.070
On the other hand, is this
mostly a storage system?

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:34.280
And you're storing a lot of
data and less frequently doing

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:34.960
queries over it?

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:39.180
In which case, the storage is
going to be the cost driver.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:41.430
And the other thing, actually
this is very important.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.031
Not so much for
the previous one--

00:29:43.031 --> 00:29:43.530
for this.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.364
But in this case and
in the next case,

00:29:45.364 --> 00:29:47.280
not everyone is going
to use every single part

00:29:47.280 --> 00:29:48.930
of these architectures
that we design.

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:51.420
We have customers who have
implemented all of them,

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.120
but not necessarily every
part of every architecture

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:55.060
that you see.

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:58.676
So in this case, let's say
you have no use for machine

00:29:58.676 --> 00:29:59.550
learning out of this.

00:29:59.550 --> 00:30:02.220
Or, you don't really want
to use data lab, or whatever

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:05.970
the case may be, that
significantly decreases

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:08.400
the level of complexity
in terms of figuring out

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:11.280
what this is going to cost.

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:14.590
And then finally,
complex event processing.

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:17.880
So I think this architecture
is very appropriately named.

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:20.190
There's a lot going on here.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:23.820
But I think we can use what
we talk about in the first two

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:26.490
to kind of help figure
out what's going on here

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:32.730
and give yourself clues to be
able to plan out how to price

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:33.990
this out appropriately.

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:37.527
So first off, we have the
amount of data that's inbound.

00:30:37.527 --> 00:30:40.110
So the rate of ingest is always
going to be a big cost driver.

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:41.901
That's going to affect
how you use Pub/Sub.

00:30:41.901 --> 00:30:45.780
It's going to affect how you
do streaming jobs on Dataflow.

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:50.169
And then, on the back end,
how much are you querying?

00:30:50.169 --> 00:30:51.210
How much are you storing?

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:53.520
How much are you using this for?

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:55.680
How long are you
keeping this storage?

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:58.345
Is this long term,
the final destination?

00:30:58.345 --> 00:30:59.970
Is this something
that needs to be held

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:01.800
onto for regulatory reasons?

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:04.930
Is this something you need
to keep for whatever reason?

00:31:04.930 --> 00:31:06.450
Maybe you need to
keep it forever.

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.075
In which case, storage is going
to incrementally just take over

00:31:09.075 --> 00:31:10.459
the cost of everything else.

00:31:10.459 --> 00:31:12.000
Or, is this some
sort of system where

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:12.900
we're processing something.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:14.640
We're getting some sort
of analytics out of it.

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:15.760
We're getting some
sort of result.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:17.176
And then it's being
aged off later

00:31:17.176 --> 00:31:21.420
on to, say, archival storage
classes and cloud storage.

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.460
And so those kinds
of things are going

00:31:23.460 --> 00:31:26.550
to be key to be able to
understand what is going

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.140
to be the big cost driver here.

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.906
And again, like
the previous one,

00:31:30.906 --> 00:31:32.280
while we have
customers that have

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:34.880
deployed this particular
architecture, we have not--

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:37.380
actually, we probably do have
somebody who's done all of it.

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:40.140
But for the most part, people
don't necessarily do all of it.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:43.230
This is one in particular
that's very much

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:44.970
a smorgasbord of architecture.

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:48.090
You're going to pick the
parts that make sense for you.

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:49.920
Maybe you don't need
batch processing

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:50.880
coming out of your data center.

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:52.650
Or, maybe you don't have
a rules execution engine.

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:54.150
Maybe you don't want
to use Dataflow.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.520
Maybe you have nothing to
push down to downstream users.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:58.824
Whatever the case may
be, you don't necessarily

00:31:58.824 --> 00:32:00.240
have to implement
the whole thing.

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:04.110
And again, that makes the
job of appropriately pricing

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.780
this out significantly
less complicated.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:11.790
So however you do
that, I think it's

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:13.650
important to have
an understanding

00:32:13.650 --> 00:32:16.980
of the products that are
being used and how they price.

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:19.230
But I think it's a lot more
important to see them work

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.491
together before you can really
come up with an accurate

00:32:22.491 --> 00:32:24.990
representation of what you're
actually going to be spending.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:26.790
So you can say,
well, I think I'm

00:32:26.790 --> 00:32:29.909
pushing about a million messages
through Pub/Sub a month.

00:32:29.909 --> 00:32:31.950
What's that going to cost
me overall when I move?

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:33.491
Well, that's an
interesting question.

00:32:33.491 --> 00:32:35.580
But how are those
messages being used?

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:36.840
What are you doing with them?

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:38.173
How long are you retaining them?

00:32:38.173 --> 00:32:39.419
What's the policy?

00:32:39.419 --> 00:32:40.710
And where are they coming from?

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:41.800
Where are they going to?

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:44.670
It's much more difficult to
think about singular products

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:46.920
and how much they're going
to cost without finding out

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:48.220
how much they're going to do.

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:50.400
And so this is a
kind of exercise

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:53.656
that we go through on the
solution architect team.

00:32:53.656 --> 00:32:55.530
And actually, other
teams do this a lot, too.

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:56.327
Say, all right.

00:32:56.327 --> 00:32:57.910
Tell us about
everything you're using,

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:00.390
and then we can start working
together and figuring out

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:02.746
how much that's going to cost.

00:33:02.746 --> 00:33:04.620
This is all kind of part
of the process we go

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:05.828
through a lot with customers.

00:33:05.828 --> 00:33:07.590
And what we do also--

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:11.250
