00:40:31.220 --> 00:40:33.110
But one other neat
thing that I actually

00:40:33.110 --> 00:40:36.940
really like about this tool
is you can slide around.

00:40:36.940 --> 00:40:39.080
OK, what would this
cost me per day?

00:40:39.080 --> 00:40:41.720
But also, I think more usefully,
what is the quarterly cost?

00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:43.220
What is the annual cost?

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:46.100
So the annual cost for
that particular pipeline,

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:48.410
assuming all the numbers
stay relatively close,

00:40:48.410 --> 00:40:52.550
is about $60,000, which is
really not bad but very much

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:55.310
in line with the
cost that we've seen

00:40:55.310 --> 00:41:01.510
other customers do running
similarly complex data

00:41:01.510 --> 00:41:02.440
processing pipelines.

00:41:02.440 --> 00:41:04.970
So we're back.

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:11.430
So I think walking through
that exercise is helpful.

00:41:11.430 --> 00:41:13.150
I hope it's been helpful.

00:41:13.150 --> 00:41:18.060
I hope that is given you a
little more understanding.

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.340
This is my manager.

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:23.310
Like I said, we liberally
use GIFs of each other.

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:26.320
And I think this one's
hilarious as well.

00:41:26.320 --> 00:41:29.820
So that exercise,
actually that's something

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:33.330
that we very commonly do, is
walk through data processing

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:36.150
on the calculator and
help give customers

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:38.370
some sort of perspective
of what these actually

00:41:38.370 --> 00:41:42.300
mean if you start
attaching numbers to them.

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:45.410
And once you know, then,
as the saying goes,

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:46.890
knowing is half the battle.

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:49.860
But in reality, you have to face
the other half of the battle.

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:51.950
And what good is
knowing what you

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:57.560
can do if you don't know how to
control how much cost is going

00:41:57.560 --> 00:42:01.995
to go on your data
processing pipelines,

00:42:01.995 --> 00:42:02.870
on your environments.

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:05.120
Whatever you're using, you
need to be able to control.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:08.970
Otherwise, pricing it out
is kind of nice in theory,

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.240
but then you don't know what
to do and how to stop it.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:13.850
And so I'd like to
talk or I'd like

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:17.000
to finish off the talk by
just walking through ways

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:19.755
you can control how much
you spend on the cloud.

00:42:19.755 --> 00:42:21.380
And the first step
actually has nothing

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:23.060
to do with budgeting
or cost controls.

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:24.590
But actually, it's monitoring.

00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:26.299
So if you're not
monitoring environments,

00:42:26.299 --> 00:42:28.589
if you're not monitoring what
you're doing on your data

00:42:28.589 --> 00:42:30.110
processing pipelines,
you literally

00:42:30.110 --> 00:42:32.090
have no idea what's going on.

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:36.435
You have no idea what
developers are doing.

00:42:36.435 --> 00:42:38.310
You have no idea what
your systems are doing.

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:39.680
You have no idea how
much you've spent.

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:40.640
You have no idea
where you're going.

00:42:40.640 --> 00:42:42.380
You have no idea how
to do any of this.

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:44.266
And so monitoring is
absolutely essential.

00:42:44.266 --> 00:42:46.640
It's essential for a lot of
other reasons other than cost

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:49.952
control, but it's essential
for cost control as well.

00:42:49.952 --> 00:42:51.410
If you're not using
monitoring yet,

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.330
I strongly recommend
Google Stackdriver.

00:42:53.330 --> 00:42:54.590
I think it's a terrific tool.

00:42:54.590 --> 00:42:56.160
It monitors a lot
of great things.

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.814
It monitors a lot
of things on cloud.

00:42:58.814 --> 00:43:00.980
It actually monitors a lot
of things on AWS as well.

00:43:00.980 --> 00:43:03.850
You can put agents in your
data center and monitor things.

00:43:03.850 --> 00:43:06.265
So Stackdriver has a
lot of versatility.

00:43:06.265 --> 00:43:08.390
There are some terrific
third-party tools out there

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:09.880
that people use for monitoring.

00:43:09.880 --> 00:43:11.296
There have been
customers who have

00:43:11.296 --> 00:43:14.120
written their own
terrific monitoring tools.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:18.034
When I go to a customer,
really all I want to see

00:43:18.034 --> 00:43:19.700
is that somebody is
monitoring something

00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:21.482
with any tool that's competent.

00:43:21.482 --> 00:43:22.940
You're monitoring
the right things.

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:24.380
You're looking at alerts.

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:27.700
That's all I really care about.

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:30.790
So that is really a message
I can't really kind of harp

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:33.639
on enough is how important it
is to have monitoring and have

00:43:33.639 --> 00:43:35.680
monitoring for things that
matter and things that

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:38.370
are important.

00:43:38.370 --> 00:43:41.410
So that's step one.

00:43:41.410 --> 00:43:45.120
So step 2, you can actually
set budget in Google Cloud.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:47.560
You can set it in the console.

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:49.410
You can go into any project.

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:51.150
You look at how
much it was spending

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:53.310
or it spent in the last month.

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:54.450
You can set a budget there.

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:56.730
You can set budgets on
whatever number you want.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:44:00.300
And then you set alerts on
certain percentages of where

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:01.890
that spend is.

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:04.530
That way, you can actually
monitor throughout the month

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:08.130
as you spend what things
are doing, how things are

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:09.810
going, how much has been spent.

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:11.310
So it doesn't come
to you at the end

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:13.440
of the month like a surprise,
like a cellphone bill,

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:14.564
over how much you've spent.

00:44:14.564 --> 00:44:17.550
This is actually a great way
to do cost, control and cost

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:19.770
monitoring.

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:23.490
Next, you can take advantage of
some of the features built-in

00:44:23.490 --> 00:44:26.820
to the products themselves.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:28.740
So both BigQuery
and Cloud Storage

00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:33.930
have different storage
classes that cost different.

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:38.427
Cloud Storage has the
four different classes

00:44:38.427 --> 00:44:39.510
that we looked at earlier.

00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:41.009
There's multi-regional
and regional,

00:44:41.009 --> 00:44:42.806
but there's also
near-line and cold line.

00:44:42.806 --> 00:44:45.180
Near-line and cold line are
designed for archival storage

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:47.570
with increasingly rare access.

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:49.810
So near-line, we
ballpark it say,

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:53.040
do you need to see this
once a month or less?

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:57.116
Cold line is, do you need to
see this once a year or less?

00:44:57.116 --> 00:44:58.740
And those are generally
the guidelines.

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:00.150
But you can take
advantage of those

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:01.470
because they cost
significantly less.

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:02.670
If you're not
touching this data,

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.140
you're not touching
this data for a while,

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.282
you need to keep it around.

00:45:05.282 --> 00:45:06.990
Don't put it in regular
regional storage.

00:45:06.990 --> 00:45:09.570
It's a total waste of money.

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:12.630
Similarly, with BigQuery,
BigQuery ages off the data

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:15.200
into cheaper storage if you
haven't touched it for 90 days.

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:16.440
And so you can take
advantage of that.

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:18.689
It's like, if we're only
carrying relatively hot data,

00:45:18.689 --> 00:45:20.970
if we're only querying
relatively recent data,

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:22.690
then just let that age off.

00:45:22.690 --> 00:45:27.340
Don't query that if you don't
actually need to touch it.

00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:31.240
Dataflow, because it's
a unified programming

00:45:31.240 --> 00:45:35.500
model for batch or stream,
it lets you actually

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:37.310
use that versatility long term.

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:41.050
So let's say as
a company, you're

00:45:41.050 --> 00:45:45.862
processing x log data for
whatever reason in batch mode

00:45:45.862 --> 00:45:48.070
because that makes sense
when you started doing that.

00:45:48.070 --> 00:45:49.720
But actually, long
term you've grown.

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:50.660
You've switched directions.

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:51.700
You want to try something new.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:52.840
You want to try
something different.

00:45:52.840 --> 00:45:55.240
Dataflow gives you versatility
to change from batch mode

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.490
to stream mode by
changing two things.

00:45:57.490 --> 00:46:00.910
There are two differences in
these code samples and only one

00:46:00.910 --> 00:46:02.240
additional line.

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:06.100
That is enormous amounts of
developer time saved just

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:08.439
by using Dataflow so that you
have long-term versatility

00:46:08.439 --> 00:46:10.480
to change your mind, to
change what you're doing,

00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:12.090
change how you work.

00:46:12.090 --> 00:46:15.785
And then, Dataproc.

00:46:15.785 --> 00:46:17.660
One thing I think that
kind of goes unnoticed

00:46:17.660 --> 00:46:22.100
is that because it just sits
on top of Compute Engine,

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:26.540
it uses all of Google Compute
Engine's storage methods.

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:30.430
And so sustained use
discounts, per minute billing,

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:32.694
preemptable VMs
are all just ready

00:46:32.694 --> 00:46:33.860
out of the box for Dataproc.

00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:34.944
There's nothing to enable.

00:46:34.944 --> 00:46:36.026
There's nothing to change.

00:46:36.026 --> 00:46:36.800
Nothing to switch.

00:46:36.800 --> 00:46:41.950
And so you can take advantage
of those cost features

00:46:41.950 --> 00:46:42.950
whenever you want there.

00:46:45.510 --> 00:46:48.390
Finally, you can control
the rate of access

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:50.200
with different tools.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:51.810
And so let's say
you're processing

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:55.442
in some logs again
from somewhere

00:46:55.442 --> 00:46:56.400
that you don't control.

00:46:56.400 --> 00:47:00.420
Somebody that-- an external
third party is feeding you data

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:02.130
that is not under your control.

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:04.980
How do you control how
quickly that comes in?

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:06.660
What if they send
way too much for you

00:47:06.660 --> 00:47:08.632
to handle at a
certain cost level

00:47:08.632 --> 00:47:09.840
that you're comfortable with?

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:13.020
Well, with Pub/Sub, you can set
up a topic that's a pull topic.

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:15.330
And when the data gets
sent into Pub/Sub,

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:17.760
Pub/Sub can control how
quickly to acknowledge and say,

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:19.260
yes, I've received your message.

00:47:19.260 --> 00:47:20.210
Send me another one.

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:23.310
And so you can actually
control the rate of ingest just

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:26.520
with Pub/Sub with the tooling
that's already in there.

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:28.429
And then with
BigQuery, let's say,

00:47:28.429 --> 00:47:29.970
for example, you
drop a huge data set

00:47:29.970 --> 00:47:32.160
into a couple of tables.

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:35.970
And you want to let your
analyst loose on it.

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:36.750
And say, OK, guys.

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:37.590
Query all you want.

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:39.660
But you actually
don't want to spend as

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:41.100
much as say they want to query.

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:42.732
They're going to go, well, OK.

00:47:42.732 --> 00:47:43.440
Is there a limit?

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:44.356
Yes, there is a limit.

00:47:44.356 --> 00:47:48.060
And you can actually set quotas
for how much querying people

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:51.390
can do inside of BigQuery.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:52.720
You can do it across all users.

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:53.761
You can do it by project.

00:47:53.761 --> 00:47:56.100
And so that's a way of
controlling the cost

00:47:56.100 --> 00:47:58.260
and controlling
what you're spending

00:47:58.260 --> 00:48:00.720
on your different products.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:05.900
So I hope that this kind
of gives you some idea

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:10.550
and gets your mind kind
of churning and thinking

00:48:10.550 --> 00:48:12.260
about the different
ways to kind of get

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:15.110
the best value for what you
spend out of data processing

00:48:15.110 --> 00:48:16.790
pipelines.

00:48:16.790 --> 00:48:18.770
Really, it's important
to understand

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:21.230
what the products do first,
but then how they interact

00:48:21.230 --> 00:48:25.580
with each other, to really
understand and really get

00:48:25.580 --> 00:48:29.860
the best ways to budget
out what they are doing.

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:33.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

